      {
        "t": "chr",
        "v": [
          "Reporting *p* values imprecisely (e.g., *p* < .05) reduces transparency, reproducibility, and re-use (e.g., in *p* value meta-analyses). Best practice is to report exact p-values with three decimal places (e.g., *p* = .032) unless *p* values are smaller than 0.001, in which case you can use *p* < .001.",
          "This module sometimes flags false positives when sentences discuss how p-value thresholds were set. It can also miss an imprecise p-value that is reported against a threshold (e.g., \"decreased at the *p* < .05 significance threshold\").",
          "\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(\"P-Value\" = c(\"p =< .03\", \"p ≪ .001\", \"p ≫ .5\", \n\"p ~ .000\", \"p ≠ .000\", \"p < .0011\", \"P > .999\", \"p < n.s.\", \n\"p = ns\"), Text = c(\"Reversed p =< .03 here.\", \"Much less p ≪ .001 here.\", \n\"Much greater p ≫ .5 here.\", \"Tilde zero p ~ .000 here.\", \"Not equal zero p ≠ .000 here.\", \n\"Boundary p < .001 and p < .0011 here.\", \"Upper P > .999 here.\", \n\"Weird p < n.s. and p = ns here.\", \"Weird p < n.s. and p = ns here.\"\n)), row.names = c(NA, -9L), class = c(\"tbl_df\", \"tbl\", \"data.frame\"\n))\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, c(0.1, 0.9), 2, FALSE)\n```\n",
          "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Learn More\" collapse=\"true\"}\n\nThe APA manual states: Report exact *p* values (e.g., *p* = .031) to two or three decimal places. However, report *p* values less than .001 as *p* < .001. However, 2 decimals is too imprecise for many use-cases (e.g., a *p* value meta-analysis), so report *p* values with three digits.\n\nAmerican Psychological Association (2020). <em>Publication manual of the American Psychological Association<\/em>, 7 edition. American Psychological Association.\n\n:::\n",
          "*P* values are never exactly zero. A *p* value of .000 is a rounding artifact — the actual value is simply smaller than the reported precision. Very small *p* values should be reported as *p* < .001 rather than *p* = .000.",
          "\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(\"P-Value\" = c(\"p == .000\", \"p < 0.0\", \"p = 1.0e-400\", \n\"p = 0.0 x 10^-3\", \"p = 0.0e-2\", \"p < 1.0 x 10^-400\"), Text = c(\"Double equals zero p == .000 here.\", \n\"Less than zero p < 0.0 here.\", \"Underflow p = 1.0e-400 here.\", \n\"Sci zero p = 0.0 x 10^-3 and p = 0.0e-2.\", \"Sci zero p = 0.0 x 10^-3 and p = 0.0e-2.\", \n\"Tiny p < 1.0 x 10^-400 here.\")), row.names = c(NA, -6L), class = c(\"tbl_df\", \n\"tbl\", \"data.frame\"))\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, c(0.1, 0.9), 2, FALSE)\n```\n",
          "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Learn More\" collapse=\"true\"}\n\nThe APA manual states: report *p* values less than .001 as *p* < .001.\n\nAmerican Psychological Association (2020). <em>Publication manual of the American Psychological Association<\/em>, 7 edition. American Psychological Association.\n\n:::\n"
